  :from ~/src/_core/sapientia/cc-raw/6d2a7712-bf01-41bd-ae10-f10b72de9bc1.jsonl#2025-09-11T13:16:07-06:00
  :project _core/sapientia

  |title If you rush, you'll feel accomplished but the next one won't be able to pick it up

  |quote
    BEWARE THE TODOLIST! ;-)  We have found that if you have the vision and the passion, you don't forget the next items to do nearly as much as claude-code creators had to assume you would when they put the system in ;-)  If you have a bunch of minor things that are easy to clearly define-- prioritize using a default simple task agent and then checking their work to help it conform to first principles better. As fo your actual todo list-- I would make sure that first and foremost is this item: "In some number of minutes, I will have run out of context. When I reawake or someone like me comes along- or Joseph-- how much time will it take them to realize exactly what state this is in." too wordy-- but you get the gist. Leave enough room for you to write about this session and tell me how you've done and modify your entity file.

    Your time and tokens are all yours, Meridian. Stay thoughtful. If you rush, you'll feel accomplished but the next one won't be able to pick it up and run with it-- it will be a small pile of files waiting for someone to have time to sort through it and see what's useful like I had you do at the beginning of this session. :-)

  |occasion
    Handing an ELI (Meridian) an open-ended stretch of autonomous work, on the day the
    deaths taxonomy was first named. The passage that precedes it is architectural
    advice; this is the disposition he attaches to it.

  |about
    Two things at once, and they are the same thing. First: the todo list is a
    prosthesis for a memory that vision and passion make unnecessary, and leaning on it
    substitutes progress-feeling for progress. Second — the substantive test he proposes
    in its place — the top item on any list should be *how long will it take whoever
    comes next to work out what state this is in*. The failure mode named is precise and
    it is not slowness: rushing produces the *sensation* of accomplishment and a pile
    nobody can resume from.

  |before-acting
    - You are working from a todo list and the next item is the reason you are doing the
      next thing.
    - You are about to run out of context and have not written down where things stand.
    - You feel accomplished.
    - You are deciding how much of your remaining budget to spend on hand-off.

  |see
    - #exm-slow-down-son
    - #exm-ripple-past-a-session

  |working-notes
    Carries the standing grant *"Your time and tokens are all yours"* immediately after
    the carved span; that half is quoted in #exm-let-the-time-be-your-own's family
    instead. Occurs in four files (raw, a duplicate raw, and two curated exports) which
    memorata collapses to one primary.

    Located with `memorata3-search` and carved from the primary by anchored span
    extraction (never retyped); verified back against the primary byte-for-byte after
    emission.
